WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
How can a cell look electrically still while currents pour across it?

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:08.420
When its voltage spikes, how do you tell which ions drove it?

3
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.660
Both answers start with a fine electrode inside the cell.

4
00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:18.920
First, push a fine glass electrode inside the cell.

5
00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.320
Inside minus outside is the membrane potential.

6
00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.540
In a made-up neuron resting at minus −71 mV, the trace stays flat.

7
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.040
Yet about 200 picoamperes of potassium leave while 200 of sodium enter.

8
00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:39.320
Equal currents cancel, hiding flow.

9
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:46.280
Each ion has an equilibrium potential, where concentration push balances electrical

10
00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:51.320
pull. Driving force is membrane voltage minus that value.

11
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:58.240
Our neuron's potassium value is minus −95 mV.

12
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.600
−71 sits 24 mV above it, so potassium is pushed out.

13
00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:09.160
If voltage moves, driving force moves, so current changes even when channels do not.

14
00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:14.473
Voltage clamp breaks that tie by holding membrane voltage fixed.

15
00:01:15.673 --> 00:01:19.913
You pick a voltage, and the electrode injects whatever current keeps it there.

16
00:01:21.113 --> 00:01:25.633
That injected current matches the current crossing the membrane, so you measure it.

17
00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:31.793
With voltage fixed, driving force stays constant, so current changes track the

18
00:01:31.793 --> 00:01:32.793
channels themselves.

19
00:01:33.817 --> 00:01:38.077
Holding voltage fixed also tests whether voltage causes a change.

20
00:01:39.377 --> 00:01:44.157
In several sea urchin species, an unfertilized egg is negative inside.

21
00:01:45.537 --> 00:01:50.377
After one sperm fuses, voltage swings positive, and a second rarely enters.

22
00:01:51.777 --> 00:01:54.937
Suppose you clamp an egg at any voltage, then add sperm.

23
00:01:56.217 --> 00:01:59.397
What two results would show positive voltage blocks entry?

24
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:09.400
Timing alone cannot prove voltage is the barrier, so test both ways.

25
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.780
First, hold an unfertilized egg positive before adding sperm; entry should fail.

26
00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.080
Second, hold it negative as sperm arrive; extra sperm should enter.

27
00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:27.830
Limits matter: one other sea urchin showed no electrical block, and reviews conclude

28
00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:29.050
mouse eggs use none.

29
00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:35.610
Fatt and Katz recorded small random blips inside frog muscle where the nerve ends.

30
00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:40.710
Move the electrode away from that endplate, and the blips shrink and fade.

31
00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.750
So they arise at the nerve ending, not the electrode.

32
00:02:45.933 --> 00:02:52.593
In 1939, Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley pushed an electrode down a squid giant axon.

33
00:02:53.853 --> 00:02:56.653
Theory said the impulse's voltage should stop near zero.

34
00:02:57.993 --> 00:03:02.273
Instead, at the peak of the action potential, the inside became positive.

35
00:03:03.417 --> 00:03:09.597
In 1977, Hudspeth and Corey pushed the sensing hair bundles of bullfrog inner-ear

36
00:03:09.597 --> 00:03:11.597
cells while recording inside.

37
00:03:12.937 --> 00:03:18.197
The voltage grew smoothly with distance, up to about 15 mV, in that frog

38
00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:19.197
preparation.

39
00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:26.290
In 1980, Brown and DiFrancesco studied rabbit sinoatrial node tissue, the heart's

40
00:03:26.290 --> 00:03:32.350
pacemaker. They recorded a current that flows over the pacemaker voltage range, and

41
00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:33.930
adrenaline increased it.

42
00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:38.430
Read voltage inside; hold it steady to expose the currents.
